# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import UICore   // and Themes, which it re-exports

struct Painter {
  init(theme: Theme, fonts: Fonts, scale: Float = 1,
       cache: GlyphCache = GlyphCache())
  var theme: Theme { get }
  var list: DisplayList { get }
  mutating func shape(_ x: Float, _ y: Float, _ width: Float,
                      _ height: Float, _ style: Style)
  func snapped(_ x: Float, _ y: Float, _ width: Float, _ height: Float)
    -> (x: Float, y: Float, width: Float, height: Float)
  func hairline(_ width: Float) -> Float
  var contrast: TextContrast { get }   // the theme's text.contrast
  func render(into canvas: inout Canvas)
}

struct UI {
  init(painter: Painter, width: Float, height: Float)
  mutating func begin(_ input: FrameInput, axis: Axis = .vertical)
  mutating func end()
  mutating func label(_ id: String, _ text: String,
                      class name: String = "label")
  mutating func button(_ id: String, _ text: String,
                       class name: String = "button") -> Bool
  mutating func open(_ id: String, class name: String = "default",
                     width: Size = .children(), ...)
  mutating func close()
  var changed: Bool { get }
}

struct Fonts {
  init()
  init(theme: Theme, from candidates: [Font])
  mutating func add(_ font: Font, role: FontRole, weight: Int)
  func has(_ role: FontRole) -> Bool
}

struct UIQuad { ... }        // 64 bytes, the GPU shader's layout
struct DisplayList { var quads: [UIQuad] ... }
```

# DESCRIPTION

UICore is the UI kit's tier 0 core: what an app's widgets are, how they are laid out, and how they are drawn, in a theme (theme(6), themes(2)).
It calls nothing in the system, so a service or a host tool links it as an app does.

`UI` is immediate mode: each frame the app calls its widgets again (`label`, `button`, `open` and `close` around a container, `custom`, `spacer`, and the tables, menus, scrollbars, fields and text areas), each keyed by an id.
What must outlive a frame, the rectangle a box had and whether it is hot or pressed, is kept by key underneath; a box not built in a frame loses it.
Layout is two passes, sizes then positions, each box's size on an axis being pixels, its text's, a fraction of its parent's, or its children's, with a strictness that says how far it gives when there is too little room.
`begin` takes the frame's input (the pointer, keys, wheel); `end` compares the frame's display list with the last, and `changed` is false if nothing differs, so an idle app presents nothing.

Every widget takes a style class (`button.primary`, `table.row.selected`), looked up in the theme with dot fallback; pressed is the class with `.held`.
`Chrome` draws the system's decorations from the theme: windows' tabs and bodies, desktop icons, the Deskbar.
`KitFrame.draw` draws the kit frame, every control in one drawing from the theme's parts, `KitFrame.width` by `KitFrame.height` logical pixels: windows focused and behind with their menu bars, buttons (the default, one disabled), checks and radios (`check`, `radio`), a pop-up (`popup`) and a field, a slider (`slider`, `slider.fill`, `slider.knob`), a progress bar (`progress`, `progress.bar`), a group (`group`, `group.legend`) and an LCD (`lcd`), tabs (`tabs.tab`, `tabs.tab.on`), a scrollbar and a menu (`menu`, `menu.item`, `menu.key`, `menu.rule`).
UICore's tests draw it for each shipped theme at 100%, 150%, 200% and 300%, check that its outlines and bevels are whole device pixels of one colour, and compare each drawing's digest with the one recorded in `ui/core/tests/kit-frame.tsv`; a drawing that differs is written to `.build/kit-frame/` to look at, and `VX_KIT_RECORD=1` records them anew.

A `Painter` turns boxes into a `DisplayList`: `UIQuad`s in draw order, each a shape (a rectangle with rounded or 45°-cut corners, filled, bordered and glowing past its edge, drawn from its signed distance), a glyph (from the glyph atlas, as coverage or a distance field), or an image (an icon or a picture), with clip rectangles beside them.
A shape's materials (theme(6)) are quads of their own over its area: a gradient, a bevel's band lit from the top left in `bevel.light` and shaded in `bevel.dark` (a groove is inset outside and raised within), and stripes, checks or dots; a shadow is the shape again, offset, solid or blurred as a glow. The border is drawn over the fill and the gradients, and the bevels inside it.
Its scale is the output's (1 to 3), and everything is given in logical pixels; a shape's edges and every border and bevel are snapped to whole device pixels, a one-pixel bevel one crisp pixel at 100%, two at 150% and 200% and three at 300%, never a blend across two (`snapped`, `hairline`).
`render` draws the list on the CPU into a `Canvas`; the GPU's renderer reads the same list.
Every blend is in linear light (light(2)): a shape's edge, border and glow, glyphs, icons, a gradient's steps, and pictures scaled; glyphs take the theme's `text.contrast` first, which `contrast` gives.

A Painter sets text in `Fonts`, faces by role (`display`, `body`, `mono`, `ui`) and weight, the nearest weight answering; a character the role's face lacks is looked for in the others.
`Fonts(theme:from:)` takes the faces the theme's typefaces name from those given: for each role, the faces of its family (its name, or its name and a width, as "Archivo SemiCondensed" is Archivo's) at the width nearest the typeface's, every weight of them; `has` says whether a role found one.
Kit's programs choose this way from `/lib/fonts` (kit(2)), and UICore's tests check that the faces chosen from data/fonts draw each shipped theme's kit frame as recorded.

Icons are vectors from the storyboard's sheet (iconsgen(1)), drawn at their size in an edge and an emblem colour over the desk.
Each frame also builds an accessibility tree (AccessTree): every widget's role, label, value and states, and its packed form for a reader.

